Synthesis of MOF- 801: MOF-801, commonly known as ZrFumarate MOF, has been synthesized according to the  procedure published earlier.57 Briefly, $Z r C l_{4}$ (2.585 mmol, 1 eq) was dissolved in $50\mathrm{ml}$ water. Then, formic acid (258.5 mmol, 100 eq) as a modulator and fumaric acid (7.75 mmol, 3 eq) as a linker molecule was added to the solution of the metal precursor. The reaction mixture transferred into a Teflon-lined pressure vessel and heated at $\boldsymbol{120^{\circ}C}$ for $24\ h$ . White precipitate was collected by centrifugation and washed with ample water and ethanol. Finally, the product was dried at overnight $\boldsymbol{100^{\circ}C}$ .
Synthesis of $Z r O(O H)_{2}$ and $Z r O_{2}$ : Zirconium oxyhydroxide were synthesized as reported elsewhere.37 Concentrated $N_{4}O H$ was added into aqueous solution $(100\up g/\mathsf{L})$ of $Z r0C l_{2}{\cdot}8H_{2}O$ with vigorous stirring to adjust the solution pH in between 9 to 10. Resulting emulsion was aged for $24\ h,$ filtered and then thoroughly washed with water. Solid precipitate dried at $\boldsymbol{100^{\circ}C}$ overnight to get $Z r O(O H)_{2}$ . $Z r O_{2}$ was prepared by calcination of $Z r{\mathsf{O}}({\mathsf{O H}})_{2}$ at ${500}^{\circ}{\mathsf{C}}$ for $5h$ in air.
## 2.3 Catalyst characterization
Powder diffraction patterns were obtained by Rigaku diffractometer using Ni-filtered Cu Kα- radiation $(40\mathrm{\kV},\ 30\mathrm{\mA},$ , $\lambda{=}1.5406$ Å). The $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ adsorption-desorption isotherms were measured at $77~\mathsf{K}$ using a Micromeritics Tristar 3000. The samples were dehydrated under vacuum at $423\ k$ for $12\mathsf{h}$ prior to analysis. The specific surface areas were evaluated using the BrunauerEmmett-Teller (BET) method and the pore volume was determined by the single point method at $p/p_{0}=0.99$ . The micropore size distribution was determined from Ar sorption techniques using the Horvath-Kawazoe method. Thermal gravimetric analysis (TGA) of the catalysts was performed on a Sinco TGA-N 1000 thermal analyzer. The samples were run at a heating rate of $5^{\circ}C/\min$ in the range of 25 to ${700}^{\circ}{\mathsf{C}}$ under constant flow of nitrogen at $20~\mathrm{ml/min}$ FTIR spectra were recorded on a Nicolet FTIR spectrometer (MAGNA-IR 560) using KBr pellets. Morphological properties of the catalysts were studied by scanning electron microscopy (SEM) (Tescan Mira 3 LMU FEG with accelerating voltage of $10\ \mathsf{k V}.$ ). Acidic and basic properties of the catalysts were measured using $N{\sf H}_{3}$ -TPD and ${C O}_{2}{-}{\mathsf{T P D}}$ , respectively. TPD profiles of catalysts were measured on a Micromeritics AutoChem II 2920 V3.05 apparatus equipped with a thermal conductivity detector. Samples were activated at $\boldsymbol{150^{\circ}C}$ for 12h in a helium flow, prior to the adsorption step. Subsequently, activated samples were exposed to $N H_{3}$ or ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ gases at ${50}^{\circ}{\mathsf{C}}$ for 30 minute with a flow rate of $50m L/\min$ . Initially, physically adsorbed $N H_{3}$ or ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ gases were removed by purging with helium gas for $1\mathfrak{h}$ at the same temperature and flow rate. TPD data were recorded from $50^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ to $300^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ with heating rate of $5^{\circ}C/\min$ . Inductively coupled plasma (ICP) analysis was used to determine the metal leaching from the Zr-MOFs. Elemental analysis (EA) was performed to determine the carbon and hydrogen content in the catalyst.
